    "created_at": "2020-04-17T15:35:45Z",
    "updated_at": "2020-04-17T15:35:45Z",
    "body": "Hi @domenic. I agree this is something that browsers don't appear to expose to screen readers and/or screen readers don't appear to act upon. However, my personal experience is that this sort of \"help pop-up\" technique is not widely used, and I'm not sure of any other reason why browsers would want to expose information on activity in non-current tabs to assistive technologies (so that code path may not exist). Thus I'm not sure a technical solution to the problem is feasible.\r\n\r\nThe spec is not the cause of the issue, for sure, but given that it already contains an example of how `opener` links could be used, I thought it might be an appropriate place to raise awareness of the accessibility implications of the technique.\r\n\r\nI realise that lines have to be drawn at some point as to what should go in, though. Would it make a difference if we were able to determine that browsers are definitely _not_ exposing the navigation information? (If so, I can look into it further.)",
    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
